8b4e619063cbf2b7fccd5bc596cb2.png)\n\nSo, in this case, the interior of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-2<x<0,\\,0<y<1\\,\\}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "cbd027d6-1f1e-5c1e-908b-e26efca43cff" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430778}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430780 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281303 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21a437c9-898a-560e-9410-f1b54bde1b49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-1\\le y\\le 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278896} {:answer/id #uuid "457a4dac-7b09-53f3-a0b2-fc26b6d17f3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-1\\le y<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279212} {:answer/id #uuid "6b30a6d1-cc0f-5b57-a13e-c4a4b0eff724" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={x}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279564} {:answer/id #uuid "be011cab-f94a-5242-9219-8918bad94e5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280262} {:answer/id #uuid "ca3cde79-af5e-5be0-aee1-d2ce3550f31a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-1<y<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280365}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280365} :answer-field/id #uuid "aec5203e-7431-5285-804f-2b85f96ff86c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281134}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "45bc0b9a-3391-5849-aa6e-c42fba251f8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-161702" :question/problem "![](images/84/84806884f843154c17c338eef0f50d83cae049aece675450e21e58dade6a9f34.png)\n\nConsider the set $A$ shown above. The interior of $A$ is \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,\\bbox[5px,border: 1px solid black]{\\phantom{AAAA}}\\}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nThe set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/ce/ce268d8b396ea63227734c1edd6b876bb0fe9755fa12a3b75be90c6ef4af5af3.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/84/84780a84406a3fd0e281737b979887e0de6b1e9fe39d440f379fbb2b84e80a3b.png)\n\nSo, in this case, the interior of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}-1<y<0\\}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e882a353-3705-56ae-8672-046cb8d6e7ed"}] :activity/title "Interiors and Boundaries of Sets" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430775}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442853} :activity/id #uuid "f8eec0d0-f78f-5fc0-b443-6a20c34d9a4c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048621 :topic/id #uuid "af65e753-6f2e-5d89-8c0d-d3d5df2fc680" :topic/math-academy-id 4063} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442851 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281284 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fa99ca1-0112-5944-8107-a465cf0627dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449976} {:answer/id #uuid "15a110f6-eccf-56f5-998c-8997e94f0e65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449977}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186449976} :answer-field/id #uuid "064a7a52-cec6-5ccc-a004-9c6fecd644e0" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450942} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "327300e8-f0e1-5c3f-a6e7-beb22904b216" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449978} {:answer/id #uuid "50af4e2e-bd80-5f87-9f7f-30c9a6d776f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449979}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186449979} :answer-field/id #uuid "72039e1e-3581-51dc-a5b8-57794c0a1d42" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450943} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3d1b1a8-bff4-538e-9246-7df95c62a467" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449980} {:answer/id #uuid "22eb3a74-db0a-5456-85b9-2111511cf1ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449981}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186449981} :answer-field/id #uuid "1116eaca-e1e8-5e9d-879d-508f9c37e751" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450944}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "46773dfb-2950-5619-a7ba-c3559b8d83bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-161652" :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,1<{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<4\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $∂A\\cap A\\ne ∅$\n\n- **II.** $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅$\n\n- **III.** $A=\\text{int}\\,A$" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/22/2273cd843c4bfaeaff10d49d3400d7661262673639a66034638d805eef5b1e47.png)\n\n   \n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/5e/5e473584ef5193b434ff6c138b08c4fbd9111a37252019143b1afb2d48c913c6.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false. As we've seen, $A$ contains none of its boundary points, so we must have  $∂A\\cap A=∅.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅,$ where $∅$ denotes the empty set.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $A$ contains none of its boundary points, we must have $A=\\text{int}\\,A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III  only.\""} :step/id #uuid "909e433a-8b41-5a26-89ce-2bbce68c19cf" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442854}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442852 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278580 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1e2b183-1fbb-5d3a-95b8-ba5907b77381" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449964} {:answer/id #uuid "009859f9-d5f0-5f12-8808-874a29b0d49a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449965}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186449965} :answer-field/id #uuid "16e233f5-f964-5ef9-8385-488a93458b37" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450936} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38a4adba-909c-5b52-9d39-dd7435fea047" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449966} {:answer/id #uuid "8350d841-99c9-5e4f-bef6-5096ca7e6bc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449967}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186449967} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1b347b8-8595-51cb-990e-7a0cbef944cf" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450937} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c563f6e6-481a-566e-952a-6ebc338f042e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449968} {:answer/id #uuid "2b815ef2-94ab-5ef5-96a3-fca6c27ac9f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449969}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186449968} :answer-field/id #uuid "4645f2ba-58bb-53a2-860c-75230a1dd41f" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450938}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "32d0249e-396e-5abf-9c3e-a9a01870f500" :question/math-academy-id "q-161643" :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,4y>{x}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅$\n\n- **II.** $A-∂A=\\text{int}\\,A$\n\n- **III.** $∂A⊂A$" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/a3/a380742f47d97062d56b482592cd2d2961dcc40262131a99886b815c507ab6e3.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/26/26d264d702368ed6fe05577d7c34630bd16b41bdba8d206dd4db39763db02938.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅,$ where $∅$ denotes the empty set.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $A-∂A=\\text{int}\\,A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. In this case, the set $A$ contains none of its boundary points, so we must have $∂A⊈A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and  II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "c2cf7678-3eef-5b44-a81e-730e7364aaf8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442851}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442853 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193324 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "afb66ed2-895b-5f65-920c-57e9c4e712a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0<y<2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379786} {:answer/id #uuid "c89a07de-de11-5729-9c3f-59a3dce504fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1\\le x\\le 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379787} {:answer/id #uuid "27aebc95-7034-501b-b146-69c5c61f0fd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0\\le x\\le 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379788} {:answer/id #uuid "f05d76fa-181c-5c72-bf35-0a16895ff797" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0<y<1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379789} {:answer/id #uuid "bca2f207-84e6-5811-b1b2-ec4c412cc4d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1<y<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379790}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379790} :answer-field/id #uuid "0884985a-f4ec-5536-b90e-64a7f3d92f99" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379785}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "13c6da4f-5008-5951-9c9e-dc34e9db3f48" :question/math-academy-id "q-35879" :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,-1\\le y<0\\}.\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0<x<2,\\,\\bbox[5px,border: 1px solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAA}}\\,\\}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nThe set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/fc/fc71859cb4d1e58ea3d15c2da66bce014898281fe237f2e0c2396f96c84c9e79.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/ef/eff037b96b6a42120fdfa112a98d1e0749cdd56bd89acd647db448b08d7323ad.png)\n\nSo, in this case, the interior of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0<x<2,\\,-1<y<0\\,\\}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ca759e97-85ee-514a-bb82-190c512bf87c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442855}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442854 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281306 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16ed68dd-7aef-5656-ac3a-0cafe1572795" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0\\le x<2,\\text{ }0\\le y<2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278794} {:answer/id #uuid "38d4ea31-5c92-59e1-b1b8-86206c057d4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0\\le x\\le 2,\\text{ }0<y<2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279088} {:answer/id #uuid "5124a441-ffdf-5fb7-9c90-48ab336f851d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0<x<2,\\text{ }0\\le y\\le 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279331} {:answer/id #uuid "df496dda-7913-50b6-b84c-a2e72d9ca9c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0<x<2,\\text{ }0<y<2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280560} {:answer/id #uuid "eb4012d7-37e1-5799-babd-f5c1ef441092" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0\\le x\\le 2,\\text{ }0\\le y\\le 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280657}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280560} :answer-field/id #uuid "74aee92b-a504-5e91-8eb4-d4d52bb0c27d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281045}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "eb88fa68-158a-5357-aaca-59045c1933c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-161721" :question/problem "![](images/6d/6debac35386e085a0197cf9eb3f2da65cc719ace67163cad625f58c056fb8f67.png)\n\nConsider the set $A$ shown above. The interior of $A$ is \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<4,\\,\\bbox[5px,border: 1px solid black]{\\phantom{AAAA}}\\}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nThe set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/c0/c0b805c707981b9b6fcfa2ed387ad32a024fc845e6fd355a4b751ede4ad6a97e.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/69/69708aa698c38f8a29d3eb69083f893fd8fca183bd713387456624fc38b86fe9.png)\n\nSo, in this case, the interior of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<4,\\,0<x<2,\\text{ }0<y<2\\}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "cd888233-2b60-5220-b469-4008e301268f"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442855 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278573 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34114c44-99df-5eb6-abf1-299e7f8a59e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449952} {:answer/id #uuid "a7a7d464-f27e-5816-910d-97e3198e8ac3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449953}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186449952} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8c5a9d2-ddb3-5ced-9423-63314912bd6c" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450930} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d7ec702-57a1-5c3e-8b98-8dd92e48f1f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449954} {:answer/id #uuid "9b3e8b1f-44eb-5090-ac11-cc11a79bc546" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449955}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186449955} :answer-field/id #uuid "a05a88b2-59a5-5a9b-8d98-69da3d4daec3" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450931} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d950cf8-fba5-5439-a346-fb6f6919aabb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449956} {:answer/id #uuid "f65576ec-dbd6-5f03-8c29-7a62088cf6b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449957}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186449956} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef878e5f-c50a-5783-85f5-385663e493c3" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450932}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "35b15061-fc8b-55f3-8742-4ca105208a91" :question/math-academy-id "q-161624" :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,1<{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A$ contains none of its boundary points.\n\n- **II.** $A$ contains some (not all) of its boundary points.\n\n- **III.** $\\text{int}\\,A=A$" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/84/8414de2e05da43c03fae3c8b1ff2102401e990188e9fd4ee59a24308ec34b086.png)\n\n \n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nNotice that, in this case, the set $A$ contains some (not all) of its boundary points.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. As we have seen, $∂A$ consists of two concentric circles: the outer one is in $A,$ while the inner one is not.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ contains some of its boundary points, we must have $\\text{int}\\,A\\ne A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "dab0e3df-3979-5bc3-b20c-cb2469721525" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442852}}] :activity/title "Interiors and Boundaries of Sets" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442850}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116133} :activity/id #uuid "d6f7c63b-f172-5766-b56a-db8bf2a22464" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048920 :topic/id #uuid "cc887b1d-8841-507d-a99b-b7888c08a675" :topic/math-academy-id 1728} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116133 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116498 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to find the inverse of the matrix \n\n![](images/fd/fd9e9dc44e27810ef490a898a7c82ae9296eaf85ac9406cf310afe86638f71e1.svg) \n\nWe can compute the inverse of $A$ using elementary row operations. There are two key advantages to using row operations to compute the inverse of a matrix:\n\n- The method works for any invertible matrix of any size, not just $2\\times 2$ matrices.\n\n- It requires significantly less computation than some alternative methods. This is especially true for large matrices.\n\nHere, we'll focus on the $2\\times 2$ case. We use the following fact:\n\n*The row operations that transform $A$ into the identity matrix $I_2$ also transform $I_2$ into $A^{-1}$*.\n\nSo, to find the inverse of $A,$ we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1:** Write down $\\color{blue}A$ and the identity matrix $I_2$ side-by-side, as shown below:\n![](images/e5/e553bcc265698432452c8d35b79fe9dc70c25010a0bcc6874ad0302ea7033ed7.svg)\n\n**Step 2:** Apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $A$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,{I}_{2}\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 7 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\,I\\,|\\,{A}^{-1}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A^{-1}$ is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n\n![](images/b7/b7c08e9a28ebfe456a9d77ccdaf6688893dfd04ba755a989a417c8a43e80ee0c.svg)\n\nWe can easily check our result by computing $AA^{-1}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{A}^{-1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "fc38ae42-2cd4-53df-9ad5-568d8e3b5fd4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3167 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "d8efc5ed-eaaf-514d-b03a-248df406a7d5" :step/math-academy-id 17230 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116136}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116134 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115249 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115078 :question/id #uuid "7c480d6a-93a1-56ec-a75c-56402e872c92" :question/math-academy-id "e-4723"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b7f42188-bf51-5546-89fb-e33ba47c4b5e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115731} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115853} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299766} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329704} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345085} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383286} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398368} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401981} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401995} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435080}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Row Operations Required To Find the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "e874c19f-8e51-5ff3-b8d2-dd1a0540bbc2" :step/math-academy-id 17231} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116135 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115250 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115079 :question/id #uuid "e13311fb-21de-58d4-aed0-18304b48bb0c" :question/math-academy-id "e-4724"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a4b135f-df0d-5443-bb93-796f32c380c6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048742}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115849} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115850} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266081} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266082} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299700} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329703} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329705} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383279} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385599} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388209} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402002} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417379}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix Calculated Using Row Operations"} :step/id #uuid "7cf9141d-1c90-5675-a333-b0aa4e0f3cef" :step/math-academy-id 17232 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116134}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116136 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115251 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115080 :question/id #uuid "ff4b4389-d8ff-5b48-904b-ff405abb4e48" :question/math-academy-id "e-4725"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77d78574-a17c-5fc3-8c14-3aaec7753b3c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048252}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115851} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115852} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265998} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266077} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345092} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345095} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345097} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345106} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345107} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417378}] :knowledge-point/title "Transforming a 2