7fc054317f2163393853c05d71714e0c9f0424297f51b46b107e222229.svg) is the marginal mass function of ![](images/02/029615e92d8b6d5c444a4daf74a9a8d63048e8d4be5f97c8207fe7af2e189e18.svg)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- To avoid getting a zero in the denominator, this formula assumes that ![](images/f4/f40e8e149f4e148b5042516dae366a5d17bdc00e1d6e39e27ddf30ece6801d6f.svg)\n\n- If ![](images/0f/0fa3deb4b6345d48bdeeb06c02ecd3d9bcf6a68cf517412b251a2bcf1e67d07f.svg) then ![](images/ad/ad17cd2272c82a9380977e2ef9ff99fb613681da7df20c06949db320c701bc4c.svg) is not defined by the usual conditional probability formula, since the denominator is zero. In this discrete setting, we therefore restrict the formula to values of ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg) for which ![](images/1e/1ebc42b48118a4c62d7203d91e76ecc1a865f8d536cf145c82499ac8fd531cc7.svg)\n\nIn a similar manner, the conditional probability that ![](images/46/46851627a248368806191a89ed2bb59d72b83b2e352f768da26f41305e156052.svg) given that ![](images/2c/2cc57b1c8b00862c078193ea1774f4012e1513ab1a26a6d5bb806e046220a812.svg) can be calculated as\n![](images/8c/8c8bdddcd02a672d59ded44b5281cf2f24236f88b2f494730bac998a760e9b0a.svg)\n\nwhere ![](images/fd/fdb49440ceb7e11cd9ba0b0869a1e6d3565549969180830d1796db7dd00a2b96.svg) is the marginal mass function of ![](images/ed/ed6dc1fd057e3dd2207c735a562bf4db22b72d1a9380651607439f00c0440725.svg) This formula assumes that ![](images/f8/f8020df80e303daf760cd9bf4d8437cc08bc4b95b0ccf64bc7a43ccddc650f5f.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "e69256ce-d99e-579e-a7f6-fbd459a410eb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5616 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "7df83356-4053-5631-85dc-83521ae0811d" :step/math-academy-id 34578 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163011}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163011 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162076 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161876 :question/id #uuid "ddfe8dda-9ab2-5b11-af30-159a7bd32d64" :question/math-academy-id "e-9256"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "87675618-337f-5af2-8ad7-9132c080785a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048325} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050108}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162315} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363245} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363252} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363259} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363266} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363273} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427210}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Conditional Probability"} :step/id #uuid "b56cb5f1-be37-5c19-a08d-76523e8ff1db" :step/math-academy-id 34630 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163012}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163012 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162077 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161877 :question/id #uuid "531b9a19-e424-59e5-8c02-7bca4f67e110" :question/math-academy-id "e-9258"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "348dfcb8-2325-5929-b622-5423b7e37a53" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162318} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362880} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363301} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363308} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363328} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410514} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427207} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427209}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Conditional Probability Over an Interval"} :step/id #uuid "56d2a08f-a2e0-5602-b510-59f2481df9af" :step/math-academy-id 34637 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163067}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163065 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162104 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161670 :question/id #uuid "2afabb9d-bd38-50f1-9afd-710ab839163c" :question/math-academy-id "e-10049"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebabf742-2b8d-5392-a077-f34e8f4bd034" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162317} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162374} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272384} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363335} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363342} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363349} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400965} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400972} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410511} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410513} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427208}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Conditional Probability Mass Function"} :step/id #uuid "000582c8-50ec-5bfc-b34a-c1cec0a03e2c" :step/math-academy-id 37921} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163066 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162105 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161671 :question/id #uuid "0e42cf0f-f1bd-5762-a4fc-3cedec697c14" :question/math-academy-id "e-10050"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "277b8807-fb74-5ad3-a89b-92dbf90bc075" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162316} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363280} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363287} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363294} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363315} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410512} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410515}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Value of a Conditional Probability Mass Function at a Point"} :step/id #uuid "f16763f3-a2e5-5d38-9f53-ce65ea110dc5" :step/math-academy-id 37922 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163065}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163067 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163325 :tutorial/content "We can now define two more probability mass functions as follows:\n\n- The **conditional probability mass function of $X$ given that $Y=y$,** denoted $f_{X|Y}(x|y),$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf_{X|Y}(x|y) = P(X = x \\,|\\, Y = y) = \\dfrac{P(X = x, \\, Y = y)}{P(Y=y)} = \\dfrac{f(x,y)}{f_Y(y)},\n$$\n\nwhere  $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and $f_Y(y)$ is the marginal mass function of $Y.$\n\n- The **conditional probability mass function of $Y$ given that $X=x,$** denoted $f_{Y|X}(y|x),$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf_{Y|X}(y|x) = P(Y = y \\,|\\, X = x) = \\dfrac{P(X = x,\\, Y = y)}{P(X=x)} = \\dfrac{f(x,y)}{f_X(x)},\n$$\n\nwhere  $f(x,y)$ is a joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and $f_X(x)$ is the marginal mass function of $X.$\n\n**Watch out!** The conditional distribution of $X$ given $Y$ does *not* equal the conditional distribution of $Y$ given $X$. That is, in general,\n\n$$\nf_{X|Y}(x|y)  \\ne f_{Y|X}(y|x).\n$$\n\nFinally, if $X$ and $Y$ are independent, then \n\n$$\nf_{X|Y}(x|y)=f_X(x),\\qquad f_{Y|X}(y|x)=f_Y(y).\n$$\n\nIn other words, if $X$  and  $Y$ are independent, then knowing the value of one random variable does not affect the probability distribution of the other." :tutorial/id #uuid "1cbdc2c5-105c-5f9a-b4f4-7e4651bdc789" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6273 :tutorial/title "Conditional Probability Mass Functions"} :step/id #uuid "acc71c94-7794-57f0-bfff-16c3d8ed9e31" :step/math-academy-id 38019 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163066}}] :activity/title "Conditional Distributions for Discrete Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162164}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410519} :activity/id #uuid "4f58fa0a-3eab-578c-8ede-b29945f6d461" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049149 :topic/id #uuid "de945224-de03-5292-84ca-39d9ddf3f7e4" :topic/math-academy-id 3003} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410516 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410515 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36eae30e-fa0d-5135-a62d-0465c3393122" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410485} {:answer/id #uuid "500b7039-9773-5a4d-a57b-2ec78d1c6cc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410489} {:answer/id #uuid "568228f9-502e-5052-aae6-cf24f3f3e87c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410490} {:answer/id #uuid "7f2cbc96-2672-5c50-abb6-ff77349a1002" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410495} {:answer/id #uuid "b71aacf7-31c6-5c18-bf8d-c7c655c001fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410496}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410489} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9630d02-fcc6-50aa-af4a-264fb35a6574" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410509}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b1e5d993-4954-596b-9a63-c575f6c7babb" :question/math-academy-id "q-142903" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{1}{32}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{16}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{1}{16}$ | $\\frac{7}{32}$ | $\\frac{3}{32}$ |\n| $X=3$ | $\\frac{5}{32}$ | $\\frac{5}{32}$ | $\\frac{3}{32}$ |\n\nCompute ${f}_{Y|X}(3\\,|\\,3)$ given that the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." :question/worked-solution "The conditional probability mass function ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)=P(Y=y\\,|\\,X=x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we're required to compute the conditional probability\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(3\\,|\\,3)=P(Y=3\\,|\\,X=3).\n$$\n\nBy the multiplication law for conditional probability, we have\n\n$$\nP(Y=3\\,|\\,X=3)=\\frac{P(X=3,Y=3)}{P(X=3)}=\\frac{f(3,3)}{{f}_{X}(3)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal distribution of $X.$\n\nRecall that the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals. Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(3) & =f(3,1)+f(3,2)+f(3,3) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{32}+\\frac{5}{32}+\\frac{3}{32} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{32}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given table, $f(3,3)=\\frac{3}{32}.$ \n\nFinally, substituting  ${f}_{X}(3)=\\frac{13}{32}$ and $f(3,3)=\\frac{3}{32}$ in our formula above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y|X}(3\\,|\\,3) & =P(Y=3\\,|\\,X=3) \\\\  & =\\frac{f(3,3)}{{f}_{X}(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{32})}{(\\frac{13}{32})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "21b7e9f5-9b6f-59bb-8de5-73acd87de7ec" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410517}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410517 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410511 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c15b04c-20e5-50a7-9307-de1f9df589c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "| $y$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,3)$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410481} {:answer/id #uuid "309c4fa5-1b46-51a0-8421-56fe01cf985c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "| $y$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,3)$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410484} {:answer/id #uuid "57efad21-b751-5436-bc49-9e31dc13e0c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "| $y$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,3)$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410491} {:answer/id #uuid "6cfd22a8-559a-59b7-b153-5dfd12c90df1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "| $y$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,3)$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410493} {:answer/id #uuid "74305b04-7264-5ad2-accf-85820a02cffa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "| $y$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,3)$ | $\\frac{2}{5}$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $\\frac{2}{5}$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410494}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410491} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2be8906-c901-5824-8831-8ec5b219586c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410510}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "51c80586-803f-589a-a591-22c79fbdc1d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-142943" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.15$ | $0.2$ | $0.05$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.4$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ |\n\nFind the conditional probability mass function ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,3)$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." :question/worked-solution "The conditional probability mass function ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)=P(Y=y\\,|\\,X=x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we're required to compute the following set of conditional probabilities:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,3)=P(Y=y\\,|\\,X=3)\n$$\n\nBy the multiplication law for conditional probability, we have\n\n$$\nP(Y=y\\,|\\,X=3)=\\frac{P(X=3,Y=y)}{P(X=3)}=\\frac{f(3,y)}{{f}_{X}(3)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal distribution of $X.$\n\nRecall that the marginal distributions for $X$ and $Y$ correspond to the row and column totals, respectively. So, let's compute these:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=3$ | ${f}_{X}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.15$ | $0.2$ | $0.05$ | $0.4$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.4$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ | $0.6$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.55$ | $0.3$ | $0.15$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, for $y\\in {1,2,3},$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y=1\\,|\\,X=3) & =\\frac{f(3,1)}{{f}_{X}(3)}=\\frac{0.4}{0.6}=\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ P(Y=2\\,|\\,X=3) & =\\frac{f(3,2)}{{f}_{X}(3)}=\\frac{0.1}{0.6}=\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ P(Y=3\\,|\\,X=3) & =\\frac{f(3,3)}{{f}_{X}(3)}=\\frac{0.1}{0.6}=\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the conditional probability mass function can be represented by the following table:   \n\n| $y$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,3)$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ |"} :step/id #uuid "30942c86-dbc3-5faa-928f-8a3e27c6ef2a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410520}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410518 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410514 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dea9c6c-8a37-5b15-a053-45fd4f8d2f9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410483} {:answer/id #uuid "c3b9f3ed-06e4-59ef-8471-d8398ce14731" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410497} {:answer/id #uuid "d82990b6-7322-5310-a01d-936be990fc20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410501} {:answer/id #uuid "f4c7c149-9c87-52d8-aaf5-4888360f519e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410504} {:answer/id #uuid "f7a8127b-532b-5c4e-95ea-e82e38e362d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410505}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410501} :answer-field/id #uuid "395c93a7-3493-526c-8001-2fe1cf196ffc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410507}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b172bfa5-07a0-5df8-8d2d-99d558ae1db4" :question/math-academy-id "q-142896" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ |\n| $X=6$ | $0.25$ | $0$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=9$ | $0$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ |\n\nCompute $P(0<X\\le 6\\,|\\,Y=3)$ given that the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." :question/worked-solution "By the multiplication law for conditional probability, the required conditional probability is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0<X\\le 6\\,|\\,Y=3) & =\\frac{P((0<X\\le 6),Y=3)}{P(Y=3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(X=3,Y=3)+P(X=6,Y=3)}{P(Y=3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(3,3)+f(6,3)}{{f}_{Y}(3)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal distribution of $Y.$\n\nRecall that the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(3) & =f(0,3)+f(3,3)+f(6,3)+f(9,3) \\\\  & =0+0.1+0+0.1 \\\\  & =0.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given table, we have that $f(3,3)=0.1$ and $f(6,3)=0.$ \n\nFinally, substituting our values into our formula above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0<X\\le 6\\,|\\,Y=3) & =\\frac{f(3,3)+f(6,3)}{{f}_{Y}(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.1+0}{0.2} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.1}{0.2} \\\\  & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b0812b16-60e1-5ebb-80af-c271cd3f189c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410516}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410519 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410512 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4176ad50-617b-5bbe-9749-530c4518c952" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410486} {:answer/id #uuid "461964e4-b6e1-5a7d-85c6-1b999a2b0407" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.37" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410487} {:answer/id #uuid "4deb08c4-9a80-57d4-8a91-17d29d971092" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410488} {:answer/id #uuid "cb1bcdc1-72d1-5f68-bb9d-cd8c02cd1c36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410498} {:answer/id #uuid "d1106665-3f25-50f6-bf70-205dfb7d3993" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410499}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410498} :answer-field/id #uuid "38673cd4-d233-5042-adf0-a6d70114ec93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410506}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "70462da6-df40-572c-b721-518f7d0fb229" :question/math-academy-id "q-142893" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.05$ | $0.2$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.25$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ |\n\nCompute ${f}_{Y|X}(0\\,|\\,1)$ given that the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above. Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The conditional probability mass function ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)=P(Y=y\\,|\\,X=x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we're required to compute the conditional probability\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(0\\,|\\,1)=P(Y=0\\,|\\,X=1).\n$$\n\nBy the multiplication law for conditional probability, we have\n\n$$\nP(Y=0\\,|\\,X=1)=\\frac{P(X=1,Y=0)}{P(X=1)}=\\frac{f(1,0)}{{f}_{X}(1)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal distribution of $X.$\n\nRecall that the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals. Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(1) & =f(1,0)+f(1,1)+f(1,2) \\\\  & =0.25+0.1+0.25 \\\\  & =0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given table, $f(1,0)=0.25.$ \n\nFinally, substituting  ${f}_{X}(1)=0.6$ and $f(1,0)=0.25$ in our formula above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y|X}(0\\,|\\,1) & =P(Y=0\\,|\\,X=1) \\\\  & =\\frac{f(1,0)}{{f}_{X}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.25}{0.6} \\\\  & ≈0.42.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "dacbdc7c-ec48-5db6-8025-bca188ffb47d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410518}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410520 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410513 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0edd7b5b-c6e3-57a4-8be5-217568bb3909" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,4)$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410482} {:answer/id #uuid "5d0c9436-13dd-5557-a695-bb3800789fbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,4)$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410492} {:answer/id #uuid "d6519b1a-e3ae-5f03-a15a-752660368763" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,4)$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410500} {:answer/id #uuid "eb5e3e81-5bff-5014-b439-496be93167e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,4)$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410502} {:answer/id #uuid "ebb9920f-ffe3-53da-b1aa-afd4167bb93d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,4)$ | $\\frac{3}{5}$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410503}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410502} :answer-field/id #uuid "970267c4-5873-5e3f-8e48-19af831de5c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410508}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9f027a74-2cf2-5941-ad79-db682e13a988" :question/math-academy-id "q-142945" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.15$ | $0.3$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0$ | $0.2$ | $0.05$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ |\n\nFind the conditional probability mass function ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,4)$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." :question/worked-solution "The conditional probability mass function ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=P(X=x\\,|\\,Y=y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we're required to compute the following set of conditional probabilities:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,4)=P(X=x\\,|\\,Y=4)\n$$\n\nBy the multiplication law for conditional probability, we have\n\n$$\nP(X=x\\,|\\,Y=4)=\\frac{P(X=x,Y=4)}{P(Y=4)}=\\frac{f(x,4)}{{f}_{Y}(4)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal distribution of $Y.$\n\nRecall that the marginal distributions for $X$ and $Y$ correspond to the row and column totals, respectively. So, let's compute these:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | ${f}_{X}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.15$ | $0.3$ | $0.1$ | $0.55$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0$ | $0.2$ | $0.05$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ | $0.2$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.2$ | $0.6$ | $0.2$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, for $x\\in {1,2,3},$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=1\\,|\\,Y=4) & =\\frac{f(1,4)}{{f}_{Y}(4)}=\\frac{0.1}{0.2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ P(X=2\\,|\\,Y=4) & =\\frac{f(2,4)}{{f}_{Y}(4)}=\\frac{0.05}{0.2}=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ P(X=3\\,|\\,Y=4) & =\\frac{f(3,4)}{{f}_{Y}(4)}=\\frac{0.05}{0.2}=\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the conditional probability mass function can be represented by the following table:   \n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,4)$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |"} :step/id #uuid "e7c09165-5bb0-5525-a272-3f6aee4bdcee"}] :activity/title "Conditional Distributions for Discrete Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410480}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 1020.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120680} :activity/id #uuid "4f1a5ecd-cb6d-546d-a554-9b94e41a336a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050656 :topic/id #uuid "5ce2b050-dd59-54b3-bb85-3a4f08ea0365" :topic/math-academy-id 1850} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120680 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121037 :tutorial/content "The **column space** of a matrix $A$ is denoted $\\text{Col}(A)$ and is the span of the columns of $A$ (i.e., the set of all possible linear combinations of columns of $A$). For example, consider the matrix\n\n![](images/6c/6c03ab3dfd7b75c9a89defe102f33946da200fb88d26139e54c889a90467068a.svg)\nwhere we denote its columns by $\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_1$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2},$ respectively. The column space of this matrix is given by\n![](images/5f/5faad23bc77f821d42223beaca06364e38c7f0ba8a4869cb138e0e923e90b8f7.svg)\n\nNow, suppose we would like to determine whether the vector ![](images/45/45349edee356334c54ad85a6d60757a6a7833a392962086815a2436446e323dc.svg) lies in $\\textrm{Col}(A).$ In other words, we need to check if $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\textrm{Span}\\{ {\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2} \\}.$ So, we need to determine whether $\\mathbf{b}$ can be written as some linear combination of $\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_1$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the equation above can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =b \\\\ {{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}{{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{x}} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{b}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation is equivalent to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}.$ To solve the equation, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[A \\,|\\, \\mathbf{b}]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -7 & -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=2 \\\\ -7{x}_{2}=-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x_2=1.$ Substituting into the first equation, we get $x_1=-1.$\n\nSince we have a solution, the equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ is consistent, which implies that $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\textrm{Col}(A).$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $\\mathbf{b}$ as a linear combination of $\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_1$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2}\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/a3/a3965a53b3b3cfda2e11c4a68ba882631dc2763f32de67de30b4751aca76f2e5.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "f65da145-207a-50c6-b59e-8756d247cc71" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3201 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e2b22098-86e0-53ea-bbc9-fc31b60b4152" :step/math-academy-id 17489 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120681}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120681 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119810 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119621 :question/id #uuid "244793f6-41c3-5920-9264-bec32cda9c9c" :question/math-academy-id "e-4807"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c8e217c-252d-529f-a810-84787e2ee886" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048315}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120313} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120339} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247182} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247183} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247185} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317417} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342180} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408163} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430002}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining if a Vector is in the Column Space of a Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "415a39b4-7da9-55ac-afde-f8932b3c0813" :step/math-academy-id 17491 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120749}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120682 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121038 :tutorial/content "The column space is defined for matrices of *any* dimensions. In general, if ${\\color{black}\\mathbf{a}_1}, {\\color{black}\\mathbf{a}_2}, \\ldots, {\\color{black}\\mathbf{a}_n}$ are the columns of a matrix $A,$ that is to say,\n![](images/d9/d9d3fd7384b4375895d234c22c9d81f30358439f7e04ee8db055de9d5d7f1174.svg)\nthen $\\textrm{Col}(A) = \\textrm{Span} \\{ {\\color{black}\\mathbf{a}_1}, {\\color{black}\\mathbf{a}_2}, \\ldots, {\\color{black}\\mathbf{a}_n} \\}.$\n\nThe column space satisfies the following properties:\n\n- If $A$ is an ${\\color{blue}m}\\times n$ matrix, then $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^{\\color{blue}m}.$ This is because each column has ${\\color{blue}m}$ entries.\n\n- $\\mathbf{b}\\in\\textrm{Col}(A)$ if and only if the equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ is consistent (has a solution).\n\n- If the equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ is inconsistent (has no solution), then $\\mathbf{b}\\not\\in\\textrm{Col}(A).$\n\nThese hold for any $m\\times n$ matrix, and the matrix does not necessarily have to be square." :tutorial/id #uuid "f33b7df2-b0c3-5b23-b26b-50509cefd3a3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3203 :tutorial/title "Properties of the Column Space of a Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "65ce55e4-127b-59e8-96ab-fabcdc24aef9" :step/math-academy-id 17493 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120683}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120683 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119811 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119622 :question/id #uuid "12f222d8-5563-544a-8154-fb1f9406b231" :question/math-academy-id "e-4808"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "87dc8398-fb5c-564b-a796-db15ff30d72c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120314} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120515} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247191} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247192} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247198} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247199} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317403} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317416} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342189} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408162} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408164} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424110} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430001}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining if a Vector Is in the Column Space of a Larger Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "4b1277a3-a28d-5ae0-951f-c3f5b643aec1" :step/math-academy-id 17494} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120749 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119855 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119670 :question/id #uuid "b6d52e39-a8a0-5048-80a1-a342a2617e0f" :question/math-academy-id "e-6208"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a557ee8d-51a9-5387-a28c-67ffefed55b4" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120397} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120398} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247187} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247188} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247190} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323698} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342184} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342185} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408165} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430000} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481356} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481379}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Value of an Unknown Given a Vector That’s in the Column Space of a Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "b98e9e59-d7cc-545a-8c21-fb820acf8430" :step/math-academy-id 23239 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120682}}] :activity/title "The Column Space of a Matrix" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120044}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424115} :activity/id #uuid "2e13e69f-cc53-5df7-b3bb-fe0183b0f351" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050656 :topic/id #uuid "5ce2b050-dd59-54b3-bb85-3a4f08ea0365" :topic/math-academy-id 1850} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424111 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120515 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29da3f11-da36-5a93-b224-a37d8e81c149" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339496} {:answer/id #uuid "3bda870a-e008-5fcd-8921-d9b462b64cc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339661} {:answer/id #uuid "7e4be675-e0f6-5007-9ea2-797607fdb517" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340267} {:answer/id #uuid "8b618a4a-be3e-5ad8-9ca8-4d4102c61425" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "b\\notin \\text{Col}(E)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340377} {:answer/id #uuid "ff722db2-a186-58ef-bd64-b656f5cfdc12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341381}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340377} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e2b899c-9539-5cca-acdc-bf68af89936a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341434}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0554a363-6902-58e6-8a93-41a3bab0a0ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-74080" :question/problem "The matrix $E$ and the vector $b$ are given below.\nDetermine if $b\\in \\text{Col}(E).$ \nIn the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}\\cdot {x}_{4}$, where ${x}_{1}$, ${x}_{2}$, ${x}_{3}$, and ${x}_{4}$ are real numbers such that $b={x}_{1}{e}_{1}+{x}_{2}{e}_{2}+{x}_{3}{e}_{3}+{x}_{4}{e}_{4},$ and ${e}_{1}$, ${e}_{2}$, ${e}_{3}$, and ${e}_{4}$ are respective columns of $E.$\n\n$$\nE=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 7 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -16 & 28 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(E),$ we need to check if the equation $Ex=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[E\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[E\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 7 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -16 & 28 & 4 & 7 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 7 & 1 & 2 \\\\ \\text{ }0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}+7{x}_{3}+{x}_{4}=2 \\\\ 0=-1 \\\\ {x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}-{x}_{4}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get a false statement $0=-1.$ Therefore, the system is inconsistent, and we conclude that $b\\notin \\text{Col}(E).$"} :step/id #uuid "79efb5b1-7822-50f1-9bbf-67d407d6bfd3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424112}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424112 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247190 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "145aaf0d-c740-5655-ae4b-ab7ab839b1d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244501} {:answer/id #uuid "3645011b-7dde-5f85-8688-666dc335211c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244793} {:answer/id #uuid "44437890-b323-5fd5-97ac-226cbb6e05ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244911} {:answer/id #uuid "cf93de62-0950-5d23-bca5-5947fd12768b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-31" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246164} {:answer/id #uuid "e1ef9d00-cd89-59a6-b58c-1a2654314dc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246324}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244911} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa5a0518-d9be-57b6-b478-a86c9d9b1ec7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246855}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fc664822-35fb-5c04-9e53-89dd85c50706" :question/math-academy-id "q-110979" :question/problem "Consider the matrix given by $A=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $b=[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ k \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $\\text{Col}(A).$" :question/worked-solution "If $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(A),$ then the equation $Ax=b$ must be consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[A\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -3 & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 10 \\\\ 0 & -3 & k \\\\ -5 & 1 & -8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 10 \\\\ 0 & -3 & k \\\\ 0 & 6 & 42\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 10 \\\\ 0 & -3 & k \\\\ 0 & 0 & 42+2k\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=10 \\\\ -3{x}_{2}=k \\\\ 0=42+2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for this system to be consistent, we require $0=42+2k,$ which means that $k=-21.$"} :step/id #uuid "8300882e-730c-5fc3-9879-32bb95590678"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424113 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424110 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "158fd3b6-b42a-5e9e-b83d-ee548a2f6fa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "b\\notin \\text{Col}(A)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424104} {:answer/id #uuid "791e7d26-9d1f-5803-b774-f6e2358c4273" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424105} {:answer/id #uuid "a421f5cb-8c63-56cc-847e-0b15d335d55e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424106} {:answer/id #uuid "dab0c485-45d4-554d-af12-2b22d279fbd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{15}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424107} {:answer/id #uuid "ed81c627-15f5-5ac5-9a83-8720033ed743" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424108}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424105} :answer-field/id #uuid "a69e7526-7c64-51e8-b0ec-23524a78858f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424109}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b249af0d-2baa-56c1-b03f-659223bf8b34" :question/math-academy-id "q-35572" :question/problem "The matrix $A$ and the vector $b$ are given below.\nDetermine if $b\\in \\text{Col}(A).$ \nIn the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers such that $b={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3},$ and ${a}_{1}$, ${a}_{2}$, and ${a}_{3}$ are the respective columns of $A.$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(A),$ we need to check if the equation $Ax=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[A\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -4 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 12 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-6){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -12 & -6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}-8{x}_{3}=-2 \\\\ -{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=2 \\\\ -12{x}_{3}=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Substituting it into the second equation gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2}+4(\\frac{1}{2}) & =2 \\\\ -{x}_{2}+2 & =2 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, from the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3(0)-8(\\frac{1}{2}) & =-2 \\\\ {x}_{1}-4 & =-2 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we have a solution, the equation $Ax=b$ is consistent, which implies that $b\\in \\text{Col}(A).$ \n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${a}_{1},$ ${a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}=2\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}+0\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}=2\\cdot 0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=0.$"} :step/id #uuid "a4bd27de-27e6-5be6-9522-5db3c45dc351" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424111}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424114 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120397 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "826165fc-f641-5f1a-94bd-36d5b21a3d8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118497} {:answer/id #uuid "b7e517cb-5c7c-5901-9fc8-4d62de0ffa87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118498} {:answer/id #uuid "d09a578d-a4f8-5169-ae0b-3f5e8801df37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118499} {:answer/id #uuid "1c9f1214-ef5c-5445-be91-c3914d8f5865" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118500} {:answer/id #uuid "20d99621-dbd7-526e-832d-85b050d0bddd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118501}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186118500} :answer-field/id #uuid "82d94d35-2966-563a-ae36-53ee87337d99" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118496}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "573bc22a-fac9-5364-8084-060fccb61060" :question/math-academy-id "q-35567" :question/problem "Consider the matrix given by $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 8 \\\\ -2 & -11 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{aligned}].$ Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $b=[\\begin{aligned}14 \\\\ -15 \\\\ k+5\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $\\text{Col}(A).$" :question/worked-solution "If $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(A),$ then the equation $Ax=b$ must be consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[A\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 8 & 14 \\\\ -2 & -11 & -15 \\\\ 1 & 6 & k+5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 & k+5 \\\\ -2 & -11 & -15 \\\\ 3 & 8 & 14\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 & k+5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2k-5 \\\\ 3 & 8 & 14\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 & k+5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2k-5 \\\\ 0 & -10 & -3k-1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+10{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 & k+5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2k-5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 17k-51\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}=k+5 \\\\ {x}_{2}=2k-5 \\\\ 0=17k-51.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for this system to be consistent, we require $0=17k-51,$ which means that $k=3.$"} :step/id #uuid "d8211cae-1c45-5bea-b330-8b808519d1fe" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424113}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424115 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317416 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0137f4f1-683a-5532-86ac-a58609ac6c5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315122} {:answer/id #uuid "36dddeda-f4aa-5342-a56d-21d473781fec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "b\\notin \\text{Col}(E)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315436} {:answer/id #uuid "40a90e71-399a-5c76-8a51-5bf7d89f38b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315490} {:answer/id #uuid "5eed2d17-3d66-58ef-b9ca-d14343adcf9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315652} {:answer/id #uuid "fe5e2b84-4825-5e40-b36f-66a99eaaf9f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316542}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315436} :answer-field/id #uuid "4285fcd0-9fe1-5d74-91ff-bd01e5c9f93a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316697}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d2325a17-fb51-5bea-98a4-12b407e10918" :question/math-academy-id "q-34364" :question/problem "The matrix $E$ and the vector $b$ are given below.\nDetermine if $b\\in \\text{Col}(E).$ \nIn the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}\\cdot {x}_{4}$, where ${x}_{1}$, ${x}_{2}$, ${x}_{3}$, and ${x}_{4}$ are real numbers such that $b={x}_{1}{e}_{1}+{x}_{2}{e}_{2}+{x}_{3}{e}_{3}+{x}_{4}{e}_{4},$ and ${e}_{1}$, ${e}_{2}$, ${e}_{3}$, and ${e}_{4}$ are respective columns of $E.$\n\n$$\nE=[\\begin{aligned}8 & -2 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 16 & -4 & 10 & 2 \\\\ 11 & -3 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(E),$ we need to check if the equation $Ex=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[E\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[E\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -2 & 5 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 16 & -4 & 10 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 11 & -3 & 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}8 & -2 & 5 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -9 \\\\ 11 & -3 & 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}8{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3}+{x}_{4}=4 \\\\ 0=-9 \\\\ 11{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}-{x}_{4}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get a false statement $0=-9.$ Therefore, the system is inconsistent, and we conclude that $b\\notin \\text{Col}(E).$"} :step/id #uuid "fa50bb1e-1a73-5c0f-bd3c-bb3e0f4000d9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424114}}] :activity/title "The Column Space of a Matrix" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424103}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481460} :activity/id #uuid "4195ab3a-5a74-5c79-a379-9f09ccd21301" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050656 :topic/id #uuid "5ce2b050-dd59-54b3-bb85-3a4f08ea0365" :topic/math-academy-id 1850} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481445 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323698 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a289e23-c521-5e54-87e0-23820eb4b3e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321350} {:answer/id #uuid "204a0a89-e831-51e4-93db-e70840ed8600" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321388} {:answer/id #uuid "377c432d-decc-5e5a-9367-f7380ee1dac6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321568} {:answer/id #uuid "b1ae17d4-a96f-5105-94cd-190c9f5d420f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322538} {:answer/id #uuid "ea3e4ad3-76a0-55d7-9ad3-a2a4b892bc0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322980}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321388} :answer-field/id #uuid "366ee9e6-9261-50c5-be6e-dbdc9c1112d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323276}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "dfc6c6aa-ca5b-5544-805f-85d28c983b53" :question/math-academy-id "q-35562" :question/problem "Consider the matrix given by $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -7 \\\\ 1 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}].$ Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $b=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 4 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $\\text{Col}(A).$" :question/worked-solution "If $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(A),$ then the equation $Ax=b$ must be consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[A\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -7 & 7 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 5 & k\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -7 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 5 & k\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 5 & k\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 17 & k-12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-17){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k+5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=4 \\\\ {x}_{2}=-1 \\\\ 0=k+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for this system to be consistent, we require $0=k+5,$ which means that $k=-5.$"} :step/id #uuid "18815fe1-7a23-50e9-b4e6-4a08c8787198" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481475}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481449 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408162 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "78645ea0-f8bd-5f81-bf27-fac8caad6e9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "b\\notin \\text{Col}(D)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408146} {:answer/id #uuid "7acd09aa-e693-553d-8659-2bccadf0cd4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408147} {:answer/id #uuid "a9c33b38-1f8c-54fe-a1a1-31dc93958a1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408148} {:answer/id #uuid "af5fd598-f27a-56c8-bd28-250a38b55ee9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408149} {:answer/id #uuid "b263cee4-a3ad-5f42-875f-5cc9d7a2c77d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408150}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408146} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a398de2-1cab-5e15-93f0-5e8dde00197f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408160}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "06040e86-0928-550a-a9a4-3db2bc9e2e31" :question/math-academy-id "q-111046" :question/problem "The matrix $D$ and the vector $b$ are given below.\nDetermine if $b\\in \\text{Col}(D).$ \nIn the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{4}$, where ${x}_{1}$, ${x}_{2}$, ${x}_{3}$, and ${x}_{4}$ are real numbers such that $b={x}_{1}{d}_{1}+{x}_{2}{d}_{2}+{x}_{3}{d}_{3}+{x}_{4}{d}_{4},$ and ${d}_{1}$, ${d}_{2}$, ${d}_{3}$, and ${d}_{4}$ are respective columns of $D.$\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 6 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 6 & 5 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(D),$ we need to check if the equation $Dx=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[D\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[D\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 6 & 4 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 6 & 5 & -1 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 6 & 5 & -1 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}-2{x}_{4}=1 \\\\ 0=6 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{4}=-8 \\\\ {x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3}-{x}_{4}=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the  second equation, we get a false statement $0=6.$ Therefore, the system is inconsistent, and we conclude that $b\\notin \\text{Col}(D).$"} :step/id #uuid "34d92445-9658-5baa-b2e8-ecaa236d882b"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481460 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317416 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0137f4f1-683a-5532-86ac-a58609ac6c5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315122} {:answer/id #uuid "36dddeda-f4aa-5342-a56d-21d473781fec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "b\\notin \\text{Col}(E)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315436} {:answer/id #uuid "40a90e71-399a-5c76-8a51-5bf7d89f38b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315490} {:answer/id #uuid "5eed2d17-3d66-58ef-b9ca-d14343adcf9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315652} {:answer/id #uuid "fe5e2b84-4825-5e40-b36f-66a99eaaf9f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316542}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315436} :answer-field/id #uuid "4285fcd0-9fe1-5d74-91ff-bd01e5c9f93a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316697}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d2325a17-fb51-5bea-98a4-12b407e10918" :question/math-academy-id "q-34364" :question/problem "The matrix $E$ and the vector $b$ are given below.\nDetermine if $b\\in \\text{Col}(E).$ \nIn the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}\\cdot {x}_{4}$, where ${x}_{1}$, ${x}_{2}$, ${x}_{3}$, and ${x}_{4}$ are real numbers such that $b={x}_{1}{e}_{1}+{x}_{2}{e}_{2}+{x}_{3}{e}_{3}+{x}_{4}{e}_{4},$ and ${e}_{1}$, ${e}_{2}$, ${e}_{3}$, and ${e}_{4}$ are respective columns of $E.$\n\n$$\nE=[\\begin{aligned}8 & -2 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 16 & -4 & 10 & 2 \\\\ 11 & -3 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(E),$ we need to check if the equation $Ex=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[E\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[E\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -2 & 5 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 16 & -4 & 10 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 11 & -3 & 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}8 & -2 & 5 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -9 \\\\ 11 & -3 & 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}8{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3}+{x}_{4}=4 \\\\ 0=-9 \\\\ 11{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}-{x}_{4}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get a false statement $0=-9.$ Therefore, the system is inconsistent, and we conclude that $b\\notin \\text{Col}(E).$"} :step/id #uuid "86ec8e45-63d4-5d25-a4e3-e63dc0aaaa33" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481445}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481461 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481356 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2af634ac-2adf-5266-9d5e-f4ec6782ebb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480817} {:answer/id #uuid "6a7caeaf-072f-52ba-8fa7-2bd619dc7e84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480937} {:answer/id #uuid "b3cc978a-23f8-5e0c-a899-6cecd8ec9666" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481082} {:answer/id #uuid "c9ba0f1e-64d5-5b98-bd5a-3378ee864a41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481121} {:answer/id #uuid "e4fc9398-1cd8-5d45-bef7-2d3eb160e0af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481174}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480817} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f12123e-d897-5bb5-a021-652c5ffc786d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481283}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "379e6627-cf03-580e-9f81-1437be9f7987" :question/math-academy-id "q-110978" :question/problem "Consider the matrix given by $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}].$ Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $b=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 12 \\\\ k+30\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $\\text{Col}(A).$" :question/worked-solution "If $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(A),$ then the equation $Ax=b$ must be consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[A\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 5 & k+30\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 12 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & k+30\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 21 \\\\ 0 & 5 & k+30\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(\\frac{1}{3}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 5 & k+30\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-5{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k-5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=12 \\\\ {x}_{2}=7 \\\\ 0=k-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for this system to be consistent, we require $0=k-5,$ which means that $k=5.$"} :step/id #uuid "8889818c-a946-5d8d-bda5-8076c4a7ea11" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481449}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481475 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481379 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "191150d3-af6c-5369-8a94-a6bfdd39b6f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480790} {:answer/id #uuid "95981581-0e25-501f-b8a4-5a0f6cf1319e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481023} {:answer/id #uuid "ad84d374-04e9-548c-b3e0-df1326473bde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481064} {:answer/id #uuid "d3299a9a-dd1e-5fcf-906a-3fe1e9c9e612" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481134} {:answer/id #uuid "db545c16-037d-5d5e-bd83-57a3b42fa7ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481152}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481064} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a46153c-e7f9-5a37-81cc-90b7f7320e59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481270}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "66f9559c-8f7e-590d-8f2d-e6935f863326" :question/math-academy-id "q-74210" :question/problem "Consider the matrix given by $C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}].$ Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $\\text{Col}(C).$" :question/worked-solution "If $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(C),$ then the equation $Cx=b$ must be consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[C\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[C\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4 & k\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 6 \\\\ 2 & -4 & k\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & k-4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k-1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=2 \\\\ -4{x}_{2}=6 \\\\ 0=k-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for this system to be consistent, we require $0=k-1,$ which means that $k=1.$"} :step/id #uuid "ffca9ec5-be0b-5330-9fd1-5e439c5ad7d1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481461}}] :activity/title "The Column Space of a Matrix" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480732}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148276} :activity/id #uuid "9b33318d-1bab-5993-91f6-d6c8c1d3d0a0" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050991 :topic/id #uuid "7789d7fa-bfec-58c3-afd1-afcff30a8748" :topic/math-academy-id 2539} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148276 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148743 :tutorial/content "Suppose we are given the planes ${\\Pi_{{1}}}: 2x -y+z=0$ and ${\\Pi_{{2}}}: -x + y-z=1.$ First, notice that:\n\n- A normal vector to the plane ${\\Pi_{{1}}}$  is $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 2,\\:-1,\\: 1 \\rangle.$\n\n- A normal vector to the plane ${\\Pi_{{2}}}$  is $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\langle -1,\\: 1,\\: -1 \\rangle.$\n\nIf two distinct planes intersect each other, their intersection will always be a straight line. A direction vector $\\mathbf{v}$ for that line can be found by computing the cross product of the normal vectors of the two planes. \n\n![](images/0c/0cf233086eccae47e640d3599908572e2c3a9996dd84d861e4d659c03108105c.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,-1,\\,1⟩\\times ⟨-1,\\,1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =j+k \\\\  & =⟨0,1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1e276ddb-1c14-51fd-8261-8c02eb3294f0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3803 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "1ae4dc56-971c-585e-ac6e-a1d1927975cc" :step/math-academy-id 21910 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148278}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148277 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147425 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147341 :question/id #uuid "fb408a64-6d03-594d-92bd-b95c2a5c95a8" :question/math-academy-id "e-5917"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0302bad-e981-5aee-a70f-66045e7f798a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049485}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148199} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148200} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262965} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262979} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262986} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262995} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262998} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338928} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338943} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338944} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366212} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400429} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421901} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421902}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Given as the Intersection of Two Planes"} :step/id #uuid "27bcbb90-63f9-5635-829b-a801f3809b3e" :step/math-academy-id 21917 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148280}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148278 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147426 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147342 :question/id #uuid "68b206e1-07ee-5591-ac08-0109cb2063cf" :question/math-academy-id "e-5920"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b5f51ee-5bde-5e39-8d2a-e5e482be4916" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049132} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050830}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148196} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262963} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338923} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338924} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338925} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338926} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338927} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388006} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388013} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388020} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393119} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393126}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Direction Vector of a Line Given as the Intersection of Two Planes"} :step/id #uuid "0ba22f44-3353-5e3e-a64c-1e09fd59d6d9" :step/math-academy-id 21951 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148277}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148279 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147427 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147343 :question/id #uuid "ae2be3b1-50fb-5abe-9f18-002a88f1de61" :question/math-academy-id "e-5921"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a19140af-1870-5dee-86a2-6052451803d4" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148197} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148198} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262999} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263000} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263001} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263002} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263003} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265703} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265704} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338946} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338949} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338950} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338951} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366219} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413744}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intersection of Two Planes"} :step/id #uuid "e4a1265b-c056-54ef-b3c5-eaaf48e49ec3" :step/math-academy-id 21954 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148281}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148280 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148744 :tutorial/content "In general, given two planes \n\n$$\n{\\Pi_{{1}}}\\mathbin{:} \\quad a_{1}x+b_{1}y + c_{1}z+ d_1=0 \\qquad  \\text{and} \\qquad   {\\Pi_{{2}}}\\mathbin{:} \\quad a_{2}x+b_{2}y + c_{2}z+ d_2=0,\n$$\n\n we have the following cases, regarding the positions of their normal vectors  $\\mathbf{n}_1$ and $\\mathbf{n}_2 \\mathbin{:}$ \n\n- *Case 1*:  If $\\mathbf{n}_1\\nparallel \\mathbf{n}_2$ then ${\\Pi_{{1}}}$   and ${\\Pi_{{2}}}$ intersect on a straight line.\n\n- *Case 2*:  If $\\mathbf{n}_1\\parallel \\mathbf{n}_2$ but the corresponding equations of the planes are not scalar multiples of each other, then the planes are distinct and are parallel to each other.\n\n- *Case 3*:  If the corresponding coefficients of ${\\Pi_{{1}}}$ and ${\\Pi_{{2}}}$ are all proportional, then the planes are coincident." :tutorial/id #uuid "50ffbdb5-c9d0-5815-9b29-b505cde7f0fa" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3804 :tutorial/title "Intersection of Two Planes in 3D"} :step/id #uuid "cba5c95d-7e83-5872-ae95-b6832ff486a2" :step/math-academy-id 21955 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148279}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148281 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148745 :tutorial/content "Consider a line that is given by its vector equation\n![](images/ca/ca43fd13bd65c544d66a4de2801871c2a79087fd960db44696ce6068d30c8377.svg)\n\nSince $y=2$ at every point on the line, the corresponding Cartesian equations of this line are\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}2 =z+1, \\quad y=2.\n$$\n\nWe may therefore interpret this line as the intersection of the planes $\\dfrac{x-1}2 =z+1$ and $y=2$.\n\nLet's look at one more example. Given the line \n\n![](images/1a/1a97b5bcff75bc7306d14a8af3fb1f169a2e7bf865be2e7a9c6d2741eecc63a6.svg)\n\nwe can write its Cartesian equation as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-2}{-5}=\\dfrac{y-1}2 = \\dfrac{z-1}3\n$$\n\nwhich can be further written as the system of two equations:\n\n![](images/1f/1f4cce850809f3fda876dd96e1cf9c65fd873df97bbba44e291b611896c86ca4.svg)\n\nSo, again, our line can be represented as the intersection of the planes $2x+5y-9=0$ and $3y-2z-1=0$." :tutorial/id #uuid "a7303bab-ef2c-5a9b-9caa-dc6fcc7929d2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3817 :tutorial/title "The Cartesian Equation of a Line as the Intersection of Two Planes"} :step/id #uuid "8aec20bb-3034-54e2-b58d-43a8fc04ec0d" :step/math-academy-id 22145}] :activity/title "The Intersection of Two Planes" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147718}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421906} :activity/id #uuid "39b191e6-3a28-55d0-8f36-ad23b66bbcd4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050991 :topic/id #uuid "7789d7fa-bfec-58c3-afd1-afcff30a8748" :topic/math-academy-id 2539} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421903 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413744 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a139c706-0e77-54a4-accb-bfe443670e5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453922} {:answer/id #uuid "f7a0a00e-bd44-522f-87a0-1661c0717d26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453923}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453922} :answer-field/id #uuid "227c754c-4dc6-54d5-bd20-dc203c3bd56d" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454711} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86a8ed31-2bd6-5da1-bce7-520c67f0b586" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453924} {:answer/id #uuid "7ef7da89-3321-5d51-9ea4-d7214de7dcff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453925}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453925} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d5956ce-b17c-56a5-9c55-441171fc7b01" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454712} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c079efb-b705-50e6-8fa4-f8d29cbaf064" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453926} {:answer/id #uuid "313e94f5-659f-51cb-b0fd-10e36f25ce06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453927}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453926} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf7ace9d-04e4-53a1-8f3e-3670bba92643" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454713}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "abe7bafd-4069-5c23-9e34-e0cbd57e3732" :question/math-academy-id "q-71465" :question/problem "Given the two planes $x+y+z=0$ and $x+y+1=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $⟨1,1,0⟩.$\n\n- **II.** The planes intersect on a line.\n\n- **III.** The planes are coincident." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x+y+z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,1,1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x+y+1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,1,0⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,1,1⟩\\times ⟨1,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i+j \\\\  & =⟨-1,1,0⟩\\ne ⟨1,1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. Hence, the corresponding planes must intersect on a line.\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, two non-parallel planes could not be coincident either.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."} :step/id #uuid "0311a340-7168-5cea-821e-a5c70f05f78d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421907}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421904 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262998 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bbb0f75-5837-5fef-9156-1bb0626a6799" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-3,\\,b=⟨-1,1,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260264} {:answer/id #uuid "358aeb92-2af7-5b63-a3fc-98a16739711a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=6,\\,b=⟨-2,1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260355} {:answer/id #uuid "4ae1bb4f-6418-5cbe-8748-f187b44dbec8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=3,\\,b=⟨-1,2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260517} {:answer/id #uuid "83b18a92-b695-5c83-b721-b2dee329f376" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-6,\\,b=⟨-2,1,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261019} {:answer/id #uuid "db99d772-027a-5c17-b647-51f20577ed39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=5,\\,b=⟨1,2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261817}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260517} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e5261fb-a30e-5c21-b272-d448a6432184" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262235}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ad00dc96-c374-5320-be1d-ca73d7e96f79" :question/math-academy-id "q-129238" :question/problem "The intersection of the planes $x+y-z-3=0$ and $x+2y-3z+18=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{a}_{1},{a}_{2},0⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $z=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation gives  \n\n$$\nx+y-0-3=0\\,⟹\\,x=3-y,\n$$\n\nand substituting $z=0$ and $x=3-y$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3-y)+2y-3(0)+18 & =0 \\\\ 3+y+18 & =0 \\\\ y & =-21.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=3-y=3+21=24$ and $a=⟨24,-21,0⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $x+y-z-3=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,1,-1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x+2y-3z+18=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,2,-3⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,1,-1⟩\\times ⟨1,2,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i+2j+k \\\\  & =⟨-1,2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨24,-21,0⟩+t⟨-1,2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=24-21=3,$ and $b=⟨-1,2,1⟩.$"} :step/id #uuid "4b62f78b-d432-5341-a2aa-4388a4c716e4"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421905 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338949 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e2afd41-011d-50b7-bdd6-b2fe0e79319a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451519} {:answer/id #uuid "f1a8a6a1-4d09-5b9a-87bf-b8affe56cc46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451520}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451519} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4ff5205-ba68-5bf5-9b0a-caec3fa1d55c" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452607} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4573706-4ddd-5c59-ae75-f76209f442b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451521} {:answer/id #uuid "8a64a65e-cbd5-5aa2-9fc1-b39766e3b1ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451522}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451521} :answer-field/id #uuid "a02ea405-f5ad-5f66-b8b5-c7c965e15944" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452608} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86e1367f-c77e-5db8-87a9-df2724a72ab1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451523} {:answer/id #uuid "bdf169b7-e727-58e0-a834-1eca473c044e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451524}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451524} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ecb6f3e-52a7-56a2-9a87-95a8d2a508e8" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452609}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "894f2787-9676-52de-a718-3b82f8fd7a1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-71463" :question/problem "Given the two planes $3x+3y+2z=0$ and $x-y-z=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $⟨1,6,-5⟩.$\n\n- **II.** The planes are parallel.\n\n- **III.** The planes intersect on a line." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $3x+3y+2z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨3,3,2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x-y-z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,-1,-1⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨3,3,2⟩\\times ⟨1,-1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i+5j-6k \\\\  & =⟨-1,5,-6⟩\\ne ⟨1,6,-5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. As a result, the corresponding planes are not parallel either.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, two non-parallel planes in $3$-dimensional space must intersect on a line.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true."} :step/id #uuid "8993a320-0fa1-5bea-8343-47ddcfb4405b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421903}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421906 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421901 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a5b51a0-3005-5ddd-b5ab-b8d3b530fe56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{2}+{a}_{3}=2,\\,b=⟨1,-1,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421890} {:answer/id #uuid "77d58298-86f5-5da7-a6f5-5aadeb1af6ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{2}+{a}_{3}=-1,\\,b=⟨1,1,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421891} {:answer/id #uuid "782abb1a-40ca-50f3-9eb2-460b14bea776" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{2}+{a}_{3}=0,\\,b=⟨1,1,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421892} {:answer/id #uuid "8b4a733d-fbda-5f24-ba39-11bd9f15b6aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{2}+{a}_{3}=1,\\,b=⟨1,-1,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421893} {:answer/id #uuid "d67766c9-e639-50ae-a0fa-fe37c24c18e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{2}+{a}_{3}=0,\\,b=⟨1,-1,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421896}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421896} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee9b9e23-e285-51ba-8f2e-0f93320571fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421900}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3582cc5d-9e46-5e0a-8d0c-b0e74f8f5924" :question/math-academy-id "q-71349" :question/problem "The intersection of the planes $x+y-1=0$ and $x+z+1=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨0,{a}_{2},{a}_{3}⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{2}+{a}_{3},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $x=0.$ Substituting this to the first equation gives\n\n$$\n0+y-1=0\\,⟹\\,y=1,\n$$\n\nand substituting $x=0$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n0+z+1=0\\,⟹\\,z=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=⟨0,1,-1⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $x+y-1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x+z+1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,0,1⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨1,1,0⟩\\times ⟨1,0,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =i-j-k \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨0,1,-1⟩+t⟨1,-1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{2}+{a}_{3}=1+(-1)=0,$ and $b=⟨1,-1,-1⟩.$"} :step/id #uuid "b2250ab1-dcbf-5807-a3a8-ee6a40866bf6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421905}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421907 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421902 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bc5a66d-b1b1-585a-b0bc-21d19ca71687" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{2}-{a}_{3}=-18,\\,b=⟨3,9,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421889} {:answer/id #uuid "9c3c1355-587b-53a6-a6d7-2db08ae599af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{2}-{a}_{3}=-20,\\,b=⟨3,-2,-9⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421894} {:answer/id #uuid "d155552d-65e9-593d-bf4d-41010a2e061a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{2}-{a}_{3}=18,\\,b=⟨3,9,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421895} {:answer/id #uuid "e23fcb96-7207-5675-97d0-1ce03137e6ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{2}-{a}_{3}=20,\\,b=⟨3,-2,-9⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421897} {:answer/id #uuid "f9b80375-370c-5de5-b724-efe10f25c766" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{2}-{a}_{3}=10,\\,b=⟨3,-2,-9⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421898}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421897} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ea7962c-6925-55c2-86b0-52523c308e6b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421899}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "caefbcdd-6450-5b4b-b965-772f5b87b02a" :question/math-academy-id "q-129208" :question/problem "The intersection of the planes $2x+3y-3=0$ and $3x+z+19=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨0,{a}_{2},{a}_{3}⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{2}-{a}_{3},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $x=0.$ Substituting this to the first equation gives\n\n$$\n0+3y-3=0\\,⟹\\,y=1,\n$$\n\nand substituting $x=0$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n0+z+19=0\\,⟹\\,z=-19.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=⟨0,1,-19⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $2x+3y-3=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨2,3,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $3x+z+19=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨3,0,1⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨2,3,0⟩\\times ⟨3,0,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =3i-2j-9k \\\\  & =⟨3,-2,-9⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨0,1,-19⟩+t⟨3,-2,-9⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{2}-{a}_{3}=1-(-19)=20,$ and $b=⟨3,-2,-9⟩.$"} :step/id #uuid "c6d8b30b-6627-55c2-a300-1bd06da939e9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421904}}] :activity/title "The Intersection of Two Planes" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421888}]}
